1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,080
Scientists believe that a majority of the earth's bird population migrate in some fashion or other.

2
00:00:08,080 --> 00:00:11,294
Some travel seasonally for relatively short distances,

3
00:00:11,294 --> 00:00:19,100
such as birds that move from their winter habitats in lowlands to mountain tops for the summers.

4
00:00:19,100 --> 00:00:21,940
Others, like the Arctic Tern

5
00:00:22,320 --> 00:00:29,850
travel more than 25,000 miles seasonally between the northern and southern poles.

6
00:00:29,850 --> 00:00:36,280
Bird migration has been studied over many centuries through a variety of observations.

7
00:00:36,280 --> 00:00:43,530
But until relatively recently, where birds went to in the winter was considered something of a mystery.

8
00:00:43,530 --> 00:00:46,009
The lack of modern science

9
00:00:46,009 --> 00:00:53,940
and technology led to many theories that we now recognize as error-filled and even somewhat amusing.

10
00:00:53,940 --> 00:00:56,530
Take hibernation theory for example -

11
00:00:56,530 --> 00:01:03,781
two thousand years ago, it was commonly believed that when birds left an area,

12
00:01:03,781 --> 00:01:08,960
they went underwater to hibernate in the seas and oceans.

13
00:01:08,960 --> 00:01:11,518
Another theory for the regular appearance

14
00:01:11,518 --> 00:01:18,339
and disappearance of birds was that they spent winter hidden in mud till the weather changed

15
00:01:18,339 --> 00:01:20,470
and food became abundant again.

16
00:01:20,470 --> 00:01:30,174
The theory that some birds hibernate persisted until experiments were done on caged birds in the 1940s

17
00:01:30,174 --> 00:01:34,740
which demonstrated that birds have no hibernation instinct.

18
00:01:34,740 --> 00:01:37,170
One of the earliest naturalists

19
00:01:37,170 --> 00:01:41,240
and philosophers from ancient Greece was Aristotle

20
00:01:41,860 --> 00:01:44,945
who was the first writer to discuss the disappearance

21
00:01:44,945 --> 00:01:50,290
and reappearance of some bird species at certain times of year.

22
00:01:50,290 --> 00:01:53,527
He developed the theory of transmutation,

23
00:01:53,527 --> 00:02:00,540
the seasonal change of one species into another, by observing redstarts and robins.

24
00:02:00,540 --> 00:02:03,080
He observed that in the autumn

25
00:02:03,380 --> 00:02:07,613
small birds called 'redstarts' began to lose their feathers,

26
00:02:07,613 --> 00:02:12,320
which convinced Aristotle that they changed into robins for the winter

27
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:16,100
and back into redstarts in the summer.

28
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:23,556
These assumptions are understandable given that this pair of species are similar in shape,

29
00:02:23,556 --> 00:02:30,480
but are a classic example of an incorrect interpretation based on correct observations.

30
00:02:30,480 --> 00:02:36,260
The most bizarre theory was put forward by an English amateur scientist,

31
00:02:36,260 --> 00:02:39,150
Charles Morton, in the seventeenth century.

32
00:02:39,150 --> 00:02:42,620
He wrote a surprisingly well-regarded paper

33
00:02:43,000 --> 00:02:47,680
claiming that birds migrate to the moon and back every year.

34
00:02:47,680 --> 00:02:59,530
He came to this conclusion as the only logical explanation for the total disappearance of some species.

35
00:02:59,530 --> 00:03:05,086
One of the key moments in the development of migration theory came in 1822

36
00:03:05,186 --> 00:03:09,940
when a white stork was shot in Germany

37
00:03:09,940 --> 00:03:16,979
This particular stork made history because of the long spear in its neck.

38
00:03:16,979 --> 00:03:18,960
which incredibly had not killed it

39
00:03:18,960 --> 00:03:25,100
everyone immediately realised this spear was definitely not European.

40
00:03:25,100 --> 00:03:29,710
It turned out to be a spear from a tribe in Central Africa.

41
00:03:29,710 --> 00:03:34,827
This was a truly defining moment in the history of ornithology

42
00:03:34,827 --> 00:03:41,340
because it was the first evidence that storks spend their winters in sub-Saharan Africa.

43
00:03:41,340 --> 00:03:50,310
You can still see the 'arrow stork' in the Zoological Collection of the University of Rostock in Germany.

44
00:03:50,310 --> 00:03:55,220
People gradually became aware that European birds moved south in autumn

45
00:03:55,220 --> 00:04:02,809
and north in summer but didn't know much about it until the practice of catching birds

46
00:04:02,809 --> 00:04:06,380
and putting rings on their legs became established.

47
00:04:06,380 --> 00:04:13,365
Before this, very little information was available about the actual destinations of particular species

48
00:04:13,365 --> 00:04:15,860
and how they travelled there.

49
00:04:15,860 --> 00:04:25,090
People speculated that larger birds provided a kind of taxi service for smaller birds by carrying them on their backs.

50
00:04:25,090 --> 00:04:30,720
This idea came about because it seemed impossible that small birds

51
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:32,340
weighing only a few grams

52
00:04:32,660 --> 00:04:34,870
could fly over vast oceans.

53
00:04:34,870 --> 00:04:39,800
This idea was supported by observations of bird behaviour

54
00:04:40,360 --> 00:04:43,730
such as the harassment of larger birds by smaller birds.

55
00:04:43,730 --> 00:04:47,171
The development of bird ringing,

56
00:04:47,171 --> 00:04:55,430
by a Danish schoolteacher, Hans Christian Cornelius Mortensen, made many discoveries possible.

57
00:04:55,430 --> 00:05:01,536
This is still common practice today and relies upon what is known as 'recovery' -

58
00:05:01,536 --> 00:05:08,950
this is when ringed birds are found dead in the place they have migrated to, and identified.

59
00:05:08,950 --> 00:05:14,573
Huge amounts of data were gathered in the early part of the twentieth century

60
00:05:14,573 --> 00:05:16,740
and for the first time in history

61
00:05:17,080 --> 00:05:21,000
people understood where birds actually went to in winter.

62
00:05:21,000 --> 00:05:25,740
In 1931, an atlas was published showing

63
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:30,180
where the most common species of European birds migrated to.

64
00:05:30,180 --> 00:05:35,000
More recent theories about bird migration...
